      "The Odyssey is an ancient Greek epic attributed to Homer and usually dated to the late eighth or seventh century BCE. It tells how Odysseus, king of Ithaca, struggles for ten years to return home after the Trojan War, while at home his wife Penelope holds off a crowd of suitors and his son Telemachus comes of age. Its central theme is nostos, the homecoming.",
      "An epic is a long narrative poem about heroic deeds that matter to a whole people. The Odyssey is composed in dactylic hexameter, a six-beat line, and divided into 24 books. It opens with an invocation of the Muse and begins in medias res, in the middle of the action: we meet Odysseus near the end of his wanderings, and he tells the earlier adventures himself, as a flashback, to the Phaeacians in Books 9 to 12.",
      "The poem grew out of a long tradition of oral performance. In the twentieth century Milman Parry and Albert Lord, comparing Homer with living South Slavic singers, argued that bards composed as they performed by drawing on stock phrases called formulas. Recurring epithets such as 'rosy-fingered Dawn' or 'grey-eyed Athena' fit the meter and gave the singer ready building blocks; repeated type-scenes, such as feasts and arrivals, served the same purpose.",
      "The Odyssey also teaches values. Xenia, the sacred custom of hospitality between host and guest, runs through the poem: good hosts such as the Phaeacians are rewarded, while the Cyclops, who eats his guests, and the suitors, who devour Odysseus's household, violate it and are punished. Odysseus is admired less for strength than for metis, cunning intelligence, shown when he tells the Cyclops his name is 'Nobody'."
