    "instructions": "The examiner will talk with you about Greek tragedy and Sophocles' Oedipus the King. Explain in your own words how the play works and why it affects its audience. Say \"I don't know\" if you don't.",
    "passage": [
      "Greek tragedy developed in Athens in the fifth century BCE and was performed at the City Dionysia, a festival honoring the god Dionysus, where playwrights competed for a prize. Performances took place in an open-air theater; a few masked male actors played all the speaking roles, and a chorus sang and danced odes between the episodes, commenting on the action and voicing the community's fears and judgments.",
      "Sophocles' Oedipus the King, first performed around 429 BCE, opens with Thebes struck by a plague. King Oedipus, who once saved the city by solving the riddle of the Sphinx, vows to find the killer of the former king Laius, whose death has polluted the city. Step by step his investigation reveals that he himself killed Laius, his true father, and married Jocasta, his own mother, fulfilling a prophecy that both he and his parents had tried to escape.",
      "The play is a model of dramatic irony: the audience, who knew the myth, understands the truth long before Oedipus does, so his confident curses on the unknown killer fall back on himself. When Jocasta realizes the truth she takes her own life, and Oedipus blinds himself, the man who could see through riddles now unable to see at all.",
      "A century later Aristotle used this play in his Poetics as an example of the best tragic plot. He described the reversal (peripeteia), when an action produces the opposite of what was intended, and the recognition (anagnorisis), a shift from ignorance to knowledge, both of which coincide in Oedipus. The hero falls through hamartia, an error or mistake of judgment rather than simple wickedness, and the tragedy arouses pity and fear to bring about catharsis."
    ],
