    "title": "Shakespeare's Hamlet and Revenge Tragedy",
    "category": "literature",
    "subject": "Literature",
    "topic": "Elizabethan drama",
    "instructions": "The examiner will talk with you about Shakespeare's Hamlet. Explain in your own words how the play uses and changes the revenge tragedy and how Shakespeare reveals Hamlet's mind. Say \"I don't know\" if you don't.",
    "passage": [
      "William Shakespeare wrote Hamlet around 1600, for performance by his company at the Globe Theatre in London. The play belongs to the genre of revenge tragedy, popular on the Elizabethan stage since Thomas Kyd's The Spanish Tragedy. Its familiar ingredients include a murder that demands vengeance, a ghost, a hero who delays, feigned or real madness, and a final scene that ends in many deaths.",
      "Prince Hamlet of Denmark is visited by the ghost of his father, who reveals that he was murdered by his brother Claudius, now king and married to Hamlet's mother, Gertrude. Hamlet swears revenge but delays. He doubts whether the ghost is honest or a devil, and he stages a play-within-a-play, 'The Mousetrap', which re-enacts the murder so that he can watch Claudius's reaction. The play ends with the deaths of Gertrude, Claudius, Laertes and Hamlet himself, and the Norwegian prince Fortinbras takes the throne.",
      "Shakespeare gives Hamlet an unusually rich inner life, above all through soliloquies, speeches a character makes alone on stage that let the audience hear his thoughts. In the soliloquy beginning 'To be, or not to be', Hamlet weighs life against death and asks why people endure suffering rather than end it, concluding that fear of the unknown after death 'makes cowards of us all'. The soliloquies show a mind that questions, reasons and reproaches itself.",
      "Like most of Shakespeare's plays, Hamlet mixes blank verse, unrhymed lines of iambic pentameter, with prose. Noble characters in serious scenes usually speak verse, while prose often marks comic scenes, lower-status speakers or disordered states of mind; Hamlet himself shifts into prose when he plays the madman. Critics still debate why Hamlet delays: moral scruple, doubt about the evidence, melancholy, or a tendency to think instead of act."
    ],
