    "title": "The Sonnet: Petrarchan and Shakespearean Forms",
    "category": "literature",
    "subject": "Literature",
    "topic": "Poetic form",
    "instructions": "The examiner will talk with you about the sonnet. Explain in your own words how its forms are built and how structure shapes what a sonnet says. Say \"I don't know\" if you don't.",
    "passage": [
      "A sonnet is a lyric poem of fourteen lines, in English usually written in iambic pentameter, a line of five feet in which an unstressed syllable is followed by a stressed one. The form arose in thirteenth-century Sicily and was made famous by the Italian poet Petrarch, whose fourteenth-century sonnets to a woman he called Laura set the pattern of idealized, unattainable love for centuries.",
      "The Petrarchan, or Italian, sonnet divides into an octave of eight lines rhymed abbaabba and a sestet of six lines, often rhymed cdecde or cdcdcd. The octave typically raises a problem, question or feeling, and the sestet answers, resolves or reconsiders it. The shift in thought between them, usually at line nine, is called the volta, or turn.",
      "Thomas Wyatt and Henry Howard, Earl of Surrey, brought the sonnet into English in the sixteenth century. Because English has fewer rhyming words than Italian, a new pattern developed: three quatrains and a closing couplet, rhymed abab cdcd efef gg. It is called the Shakespearean, or English, sonnet after William Shakespeare, whose 154 sonnets were published in 1609. The quatrains develop an idea in stages, and the couplet often delivers a turn, summary or twist.",
      "In Sonnet 18, which begins 'Shall I compare thee to a summer's day?', Shakespeare's quatrains show that summer is brief and imperfect, then claim that the beloved's 'eternal summer shall not fade', and the couplet explains why: the poem itself will keep the beloved alive as long as people read it. Many of Shakespeare's sonnets also overturn Petrarchan conventions; Sonnet 130 mocks the habit of comparing a mistress's eyes to the sun."
    ],
    "objectives": [
      { "key": "meter", "title": "Explain the sonnet's basic form and meter", "description": "States the fourteen-line form and explains iambic pentameter.",
        "concept": "Sonnet and iambic pentameter", "definition": "A sonnet is a fourteen-line lyric poem, in English usually in iambic pentameter: five feet of unstressed then stressed syllables.",
        "facts": ["A sonnet has fourteen lines.", "An iamb is an unstressed syllable followed by a stressed one.", "Pentameter means five feet per line."],
        "misconceptions": ["Any short love poem is a sonnet.", "Iambic pentameter means a line of ten words."] },
      { "key": "petrarchan", "title": "Describe the Petrarchan sonnet", "description": "Explains the octave-sestet structure, its rhyme scheme and the volta.",
        "concept": "Petrarchan sonnet", "definition": "The Italian sonnet has an octave rhymed abbaabba and a sestet, with a volta, or turn in thought, usually at line nine.",
        "facts": ["The octave rhymes abbaabba.", "The sestet often rhymes cdecde or cdcdcd.", "The volta usually comes at line nine."],
        "misconceptions": ["The volta is a change of rhyme only, not of thought."] },
      { "key": "shakespearean", "title": "Describe the Shakespearean sonnet", "description": "Explains three quatrains and a couplet and why the form developed in English.",
        "concept": "Shakespearean sonnet", "definition": "The English sonnet has three quatrains and a final couplet rhymed abab cdcd efef gg.",
        "facts": ["It rhymes abab cdcd efef gg.", "Wyatt and Surrey brought the sonnet into English.", "English has fewer rhymes than Italian, which favored more rhyme sounds.", "The couplet often gives a turn or conclusion."],
        "misconceptions": ["Shakespeare invented the sonnet."] },
      { "key": "form-meaning", "title": "Explain how structure shapes meaning", "description": "Uses Sonnet 18 or 130 to show how the parts of a sonnet build and turn an argument.",
        "concept": "Form and meaning", "definition": "A sonnet's divisions organize its argument: problem and answer, development and turn.",
        "facts": ["In Sonnet 18 the quatrains contrast summer's decay with the beloved's lasting beauty.", "Sonnet 18's couplet claims the poem will preserve the beloved.", "Sonnet 130 parodies Petrarchan comparisons."],
        "misconceptions": ["Form is just decoration with no effect on meaning."] }
