  {
    "title": "Don Quixote and the Modern Novel",
    "category": "literature",
    "subject": "Literature",
    "topic": "Spanish Golden Age prose",
    "instructions": "The examiner will talk with you about Cervantes's Don Quixote. Explain in your own words what the book parodies, how it plays with its own telling and why it is called a founding modern novel. Say \"I don't know\" if you don't.",
    "passage": [
      "Miguel de Cervantes published Don Quixote in two parts, in 1605 and 1615, during Spain's Golden Age. Its hero is Alonso Quijano, a minor gentleman of La Mancha who reads so many romances of chivalry that he loses his grip on reality. Renaming himself Don Quixote, he sets out as a knight-errant on a worn-out horse, Rocinante, with a farmer, Sancho Panza, as his squire, and devotes his deeds to an idealized lady, Dulcinea, who is really a peasant girl he has barely seen.",
      "The book began as a parody of chivalric romances, which were hugely popular and full of enchanters, giants and impossible feats. Don Quixote sees the ordinary world through their lens: he charges at windmills he takes for giants and treats inns as castles. The comedy comes from the gap between his heroic vision and the plain reality around him, yet over the novel he also becomes a figure of dignity and idealism.",
      "Don Quixote is famously self-aware. The narrator claims the story was translated from an Arabic chronicle by a historian called Cide Hamete Benengeli, which raises questions about who is telling the story and whether he can be trusted. In Part II, characters have read Part I and recognize the knight and his squire, and Cervantes mocks a rival, unauthorized sequel published in 1614 under the name Avellaneda. Fiction that draws attention to its own status as fiction is called metafiction.",
      "Many critics call Don Quixote the first modern novel. Instead of fixed types moving through marvels, it shows characters who change through their relationship: Sancho grows wiser and more imaginative, while Quixote becomes more doubtful, a mutual influence sometimes called the 'quixotization' of Sancho and the 'sanchification' of Quixote. It sets idealism against realism and shows the same events through different perspectives, themes the novel as a form has explored ever since."
    ],
    "objectives": [
      { "key": "parody", "title": "Explain the novel as a parody of chivalric romance", "description": "Explains what chivalric romances were and how the novel mocks and transforms them.",
        "concept": "Parody", "definition": "Parody imitates the style and conventions of a genre or work in exaggerated form to mock or comment on it.",
        "facts": ["Chivalric romances were popular tales of knights, enchanters and marvels.", "Quixote takes windmills for giants and inns for castles.", "The humor comes from the gap between his vision and reality."],
