      "The Tale of Genji was written in the early eleventh century by Murasaki Shikibu, a lady-in-waiting at the imperial court of Heian Japan, in the capital Heian-kyō, today's Kyoto. A long prose narrative in 54 chapters, it follows the life and loves of Hikaru Genji, the 'Shining Genji', a brilliant and handsome son of an emperor who is removed from the line of succession and made a commoner, and it continues after his death with the lives of his descendants in the final chapters, set at Uji.",
      "Language and gender shaped Heian writing. Official documents and learned works by men were written in classical Chinese, while women such as Murasaki wrote in Japanese using kana, the phonetic script from which hiragana derives. As a result much of the period's most important vernacular literature, including Genji and Sei Shōnagon's The Pillow Book, was written by women at court.",
      "Poetry is woven through the tale. It contains some 800 waka, short poems of 31 syllables, which characters exchange to court, apologize, grieve or hint at feelings that cannot be said openly. In Heian court culture, skill in poetry, calligraphy, music and taste in dress and paper revealed a person's refinement and could decide a romance.",
      "The tale's mood is often described with the term mono no aware, 'the pathos of things', a gentle sorrow at the passing of beauty and life. The eighteenth-century scholar Motoori Norinaga made the term central to reading Genji. Because of its psychological depth, its long time span and its focus on inner experience, the work is often called one of the world's earliest novels."
    ],
