    "title": "Tang Dynasty Poetry: Li Bai and Du Fu",
    "category": "literature",
    "subject": "Literature",
    "topic": "Classical Chinese poetry",
    "instructions": "The examiner will talk with you about Tang dynasty poetry. Explain in your own words the forms Tang poets used and how Li Bai and Du Fu differ. Say \"I don't know\" if you don't.",
    "passage": [
      "The Tang dynasty (618 to 907) is often called the golden age of Chinese poetry. Poetry was part of elite life: it was written at gatherings and partings, exchanged between friends and officials, and for long periods composing verse was tested in the imperial civil service examinations. Tens of thousands of Tang poems survive.",
      "Tang poets perfected 'recent-style' forms with strict rules. Regulated verse (lüshi) has eight lines of five or seven characters each, follows set patterns of tones, and requires the two middle couplets to be parallel, matching word for word in grammar and sense. The quatrain (jueju) has four lines and is prized for compression: a single image can carry a whole mood. Older, freer forms, such as the 'old-style' poem, remained in use.",
      "Li Bai (701 to 762) is remembered as a free and romantic spirit. His poems celebrate wine, friendship, mountains and the moon, often with a Daoist sense of escape from worldly limits and a bold, spontaneous voice. His short poem known in English as 'Quiet Night Thought', in which moonlight on the floor looks like frost and turns the speaker's mind to home, is among the best known poems in China.",
      "Du Fu (712 to 770) lived through the An Lushan Rebellion (755 to 763), which devastated the empire. His poems record war, displacement and the suffering of ordinary people with a Confucian sense of moral duty, and later readers called him the 'poet-historian'. He is admired as a master of regulated verse. Both poets show how classical Chinese poetry joins a precise image to an emotion, which makes translation especially difficult, since the compressed grammar and tonal music do not carry over."
    ],
