  {
    "title": "Sijo: Korea's Classical Verse Form",
    "category": "literature",
    "subject": "Literature",
    "topic": "Classical Korean poetry",
    "instructions": "The examiner will talk with you about sijo, the classical Korean poem. Explain in your own words how a sijo is built, who wrote sijo and what they express. Say \"I don't know\" if you don't.",
    "passage": [
      "Sijo is a Korean poetic form that took shape in the late Goryeo period and flourished in the Joseon dynasty (1392 to 1910). It was originally sung to music, and for centuries it was the most widely practiced verse form in Korea. Before the Korean alphabet, hangul, was created in the fifteenth century, sijo were passed on orally or recorded with Chinese characters; afterward they could be written down directly in Korean.",
      "A sijo has three lines, each of about fourteen to sixteen syllables, for a total of around forty-three to forty-five. Each line breaks into two halves and four smaller groups of syllables. The first line introduces a situation or theme, the second develops it, and the third delivers a twist, a turn in thought, or a resolution. The third line opens with a group of exactly three syllables, which signals the turn, followed by a longer group.",
      "Sijo were written by many kinds of people. Confucian scholar-officials of the yangban class used the form to express loyalty to the king, moral principles and the pleasures of retirement in nature; Yun Seon-do's seventeenth-century cycle 'The Fisherman's Calendar' pictures a simple life on the water through the seasons. Kisaeng, trained female entertainers, also composed celebrated sijo; Hwang Jini, of the sixteenth century, is famous for poems of love and longing that play with images of time and nature.",
      "A famous exchange shows how sijo could carry political meaning. Yi Bang-won, son of the founder of Joseon, is said to have tested the loyalist Jeong Mong-ju with a sijo suggesting that, like vines tangled together, they should join the new dynasty. Jeong replied with his 'Song of a Single Heart', declaring that even if he died a hundred times his loyalty to Goryeo would not change; he was killed in 1392. Later, longer variants such as the narrative sasol sijo opened the form to humor and everyday life."
    ],
    "objectives": [
      { "key": "structure", "title": "Describe the structure of a sijo", "description": "Explains the three lines, syllable counts and the job of each line.",
        "concept": "Sijo structure", "definition": "A sijo has three lines of about fourteen to sixteen syllables: the first introduces, the second develops and the third turns or resolves.",
        "facts": ["A sijo has three lines.", "Each line has about fourteen to sixteen syllables.", "The total is around forty-three to forty-five syllables.", "Each line divides into two halves."],
        "misconceptions": ["A sijo is the same as a Japanese haiku.", "A sijo has no fixed structure."] },
      { "key": "turn", "title": "Explain the third-line turn", "description": "Explains the three-syllable opening of the third line and the twist it signals.",
        "concept": "The final turn", "definition": "The third line opens with a group of exactly three syllables that signals a twist or resolution of the poem's idea.",
        "facts": ["The third line begins with a three-syllable group.", "That group signals a turn in thought.", "The third line resolves or reframes what came before."],
        "misconceptions": ["The third line simply repeats the first."] },
      { "key": "history", "title": "Explain the history and performance of sijo", "description": "Places sijo in Goryeo and Joseon and explains its musical and written transmission.",
        "concept": "History of sijo", "definition": "Sijo arose in late Goryeo, flourished in Joseon, was sung to music and could be written in hangul after the fifteenth century.",
        "facts": ["Sijo took shape in the late Goryeo period.", "It flourished in the Joseon dynasty.", "It was originally sung.", "Hangul let sijo be written directly in Korean."],
        "misconceptions": ["Sijo was invented in the twentieth century."] },
      { "key": "poets", "title": "Explain who wrote sijo and why", "description": "Uses yangban scholars, kisaeng poets and the Jeong Mong-ju exchange as examples.",
        "concept": "Sijo poets and themes", "definition": "Sijo was written by scholar-officials on loyalty, virtue and nature and by kisaeng poets on love and longing, and could carry political meaning.",
        "facts": ["Yangban scholar-officials wrote of loyalty, morality and nature.", "Hwang Jini was a celebrated kisaeng poet.", "Jeong Mong-ju's 'Song of a Single Heart' declared loyalty to Goryeo.", "Yun Seon-do wrote 'The Fisherman's Calendar'."],
        "misconceptions": ["Only male aristocrats wrote sijo."] }
    ],
    "translations": {
