  {
    "title": "The Epic of Sundiata and the Griot Tradition",
    "category": "literature",
    "subject": "Literature",
    "topic": "West African oral literature",
    "instructions": "The examiner will talk with you about the Epic of Sundiata. Explain in your own words what the epic tells, who keeps it alive and how oral literature differs from written texts. Say \"I don't know\" if you don't.",
    "passage": [
      "The Epic of Sundiata is the founding story of the Mali Empire, told among the Mande peoples of West Africa. It recounts the life of Sundiata Keita, who in the thirteenth century united the Mande clans and defeated Soumaoro Kanté, the powerful sorcerer-king of Sosso, at the Battle of Kirina, around 1235. Sundiata became the first ruler of Mali, which grew into one of the largest empires in African history.",
      "The epic follows a classic heroic pattern. A prophecy foretells that Sundiata will become a great king, yet he is born unable to walk and mocked as a child; his mother, Sogolon, is scorned at court. When he finally rises to his feet, he shows extraordinary strength. Driven into exile by a jealous rival queen, he grows to manhood abroad, then returns at his people's call to free them from Soumaoro's tyranny, whose magical protection is overcome once his secret weakness is discovered.",
      "The epic has been preserved not in a single written text but by griots, called jeli in Mande languages. Griots are hereditary bards, historians, genealogists and advisers to rulers; they recite the deeds and lineages of families and peoples, often accompanied by instruments such as the kora, a harp-lute, or the balafon, a wooden xylophone. Their art is passed from generation to generation within griot families.",
      "As oral literature, the epic exists in many versions. Each performance adapts to its audience and occasion, and different griots and regions tell episodes differently, so there is no single authoritative text. Written and translated versions recorded in the twentieth century made the epic widely known, but they capture particular tellings. The epic shows how oral traditions preserve history, give a people its identity and teach values such as destiny, perseverance and the duties of a ruler."
    ],
    "objectives": [
      { "key": "story", "title": "Summarize the story and its history", "description": "Explains who Sundiata was and links the epic to the founding of the Mali Empire.",
